From 70220aed52a18f2997cc3be63180df57f731d44c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aryan Randeriya <aryan.k.randeriya@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:38:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 08/18] fix(js): make window a real EventTarget and run custom
 element reactions

YouTube threw "window.__shady_native_addEventListener is not a function"
from webcomponents-sd.js and rendered nothing to use (0 inputs, buttons
and links; Chrome shows 1/6/3).

Root cause: EventTarget was an alias for Node, and window carried its own
addEventListener/removeEventListener/dispatchEvent while its prototype
chain held neither Window.prototype nor EventTarget.prototype. ShadyDOM
copies the three descriptors off EventTarget.prototype as
__shady_native_*, sees window does not inherit them, falls back to
Window.prototype, finds nothing there either, and then calls
window.__shady_native_addEventListener.

Now, as in a browser, EventTarget is its own interface. Node extends it,
the global's chain is window -> Window.prototype -> EventTarget.prototype,
and only EventTarget.prototype owns the three methods. Elements and
documents keep their own listener storage, registered per prototype, and
the shared methods use the nearest one on the target's chain. A bare call
with no receiver targets window, per WebIDL.

With ShadyDOM running, three more engine gaps kept YouTube's Polymer
components from rendering:

- textContent/nodeValue on a Text or Comment appended a child instead of
  replacing its data, so no characterData record was queued. Polymer's and
  ShadyDOM's microtask queues flip a detached text node under a
  characterData MutationObserver, so their flushes never ran.
- Custom elements only reacted when their definition arrived. Inserting a
  defined element now upgrades it or calls connectedCallback, removing it
  calls disconnectedCallback, observed attribute changes call
  attributeChangedCallback (also once per attribute at upgrade), and
  importNode upgrades the defined elements it creates. This covers
  appendChild, insertBefore, replaceChild, removeChild, innerHTML on
  elements and shadow roots, and textContent. Upgrade errors are reported
  instead of swallowed.
- classList, relList, sandbox and sizes had getters only, so assigning a
  string threw in strict code ("Cannot set property sandbox of #<Element>
  which has only a getter"). They are [PutForwards=value] and now set the
  attribute.

YouTube now probes at inputs=1 buttons=6 links=7 (Chrome 1/6/3) with no
Obscura-only errors. DuckDuckGo, GitHub, Stack Overflow, Bing, Wikipedia,
npm, react.dev, Reddit and MDN probe the same as before.

